---C00---
## Differentiation within parent

Where sibling cluster 27 reframes its target as a differentiable multi-agent game whose Nash equilibrium is engineered to coincide with the desired solution — making equilibrium existence/coincidence the correctness criterion — this cluster reframes opaque model-transparency questions as a measurement: an estimator, selector, or decomposition whose output is a per-unit responsibility or uncertainty score and whose correctness criterion is fidelity-to-a-fixed-model, certified by causal intervention or a soundness proof. Sibling cluster 21 instead reframes its problem as a generative iterated-transmission / emergent-language process — a dynamics over successive learner generations rather than a static scoring of one trained artifact. The distinguishing move here is that the object being solved produces an attribution or uncertainty number about an already-trained model or dataset and is validated by perturbing that model, not by reaching an equilibrium (cluster 27) or simulating a transmission dynamic (cluster 21).

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the problem is to explain, attribute, or quantify confidence about a single already-trained model or fixed dataset and you need a faithful per-unit responsibility or uncertainty score — not when the structure is competitive/multi-party (which favors an equilibrium reframe) or generative across successive populations. The tell is a fixed artifact whose internal behavior you must measure, plus an existing measurement object (counterfactual, subset selection, decomposition, influence/Shapley, causal mediation) you can validate by perturbing the model itself.


---C21---
## Differentiation within parent

The solvable object this cluster reaches for is supervised learning or autoregressive generative modeling, and its signature sub-move is fabricating the supervision the native problem lacks — relabeling achieved outcomes as targets, or substituting reward/action labels with self-supervised proxies. Contrast cluster 27, which recasts the problem as a differentiable multi-agent game whose Nash equilibrium coincides with the desired ranked decomposition: that cluster engineers competing local objectives and reads off an equilibrium, never manufacturing missing labels and never invoking generative/supervised solvers. Contrast also cluster 0, which keeps the decision-scoring frame intact and enriches it with interpretable cumulative sub-objective predictors embedded directly in the scoring computation, rather than wholesale converting the control problem into generative-sequence training. Cluster 21 is the one where 'no rewards / no action labels / no demos' is converted into 'self-generated labels,' and the recast target is a generative or supervised learner that existing machinery can solve off the shelf.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the sequential-decision problem is blocked specifically by absent supervision — no reward signal, no action annotations, or no optimal demonstrations — yet abundant unlabeled or task-agnostic interaction data is available to manufacture targets from. If instead the blocker is competitive or allocation structure (favoring a game/equilibrium recast) or an opaque scoring computation that needs interpretable predictors, a different sibling fits better.


---C27---
## Differentiation within parent

Where sibling cluster 0 reframes by engineering a single learned decision-scoring function over interpretable auxiliary predictors — keeping one decision-maker and treating the reframing as objective design via a learned aggregation — this cluster instantiates the parent's \"game/equilibrium\" branch: it introduces MULTIPLE strategic agents and an explicit equilibrium solution concept, so the solvable object is a fixed point among competing or cooperating utilities rather than a forward-looking scoring function. And where cluster 21 ports a generative iterated-transmission / emergent-language framework into an architecture, this cluster ports the apparatus of game theory specifically — players, payoffs, equilibria, regret dynamics, complexity reductions. The distinguishing artifact is a proof that an equilibrium of a deliberately constructed game equals the target solution (plus the solver that reaches it), which neither sibling produces.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the problem can be endowed with multiple interacting decision-makers whose competing or cooperating incentives — and a stable fixed point among them — naturally express the quantity you want to compute, and when mature equilibrium-solving machinery (regret minimization, best response, complexity reductions) already exists to exploit.


---C01---
## Differentiation within parent

All siblings share the parent's audit-and-pivot signature, but they pivot mathematical objects inside a fixed locus, whereas this cluster pivots the locus itself in adversarial/empirical security and safety settings and validates with attack-success rates and detection performance rather than tighter analysis. Cluster 11 re-derives privacy-accounting bounds by reframing repeated selection as a resource-bounded mechanism — the assumption is relaxed but the quantity (a cumulative bound) and its locus (the accounting calculus) are unchanged; this cluster instead relocates the enforcement point entirely (e.g., from parameter-divergence to score-distribution geometry) and shows the old locus was the wrong place to look. Likewise cluster 29 proves a new property of a projection operator on a constraint set and cluster 5 derives an exact algebraic equivalence between model families — both are theorem-driven reformulations of analytic structure, while this cluster's deliverable is a demonstrated attack surface or a transformation-invariant detection signal. The contrast is empirical-locus-shift-under-adversary versus closed-form-quantity-tightening.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the gap sits in a security, safety, detection, or provenance setting and you can observe that every existing attack or defense is anchored at one representational locus (raw input, parameters/gradients, a scalar score, a deterministic output, or an injected artifact). The trigger to instantiate here is a suspicion that the real load-bearing signal lives at an overlooked locus — labels, activations, distribution shape, or behavior — that the field has tacitly assumed irrelevant or invariant.


---C05---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster's pivot targets an IDENTIFIABILITY assumption and re-proves recovery of hidden latent, causal, or generative structure up to an ambiguity class — the deliverable is a recovery theorem with a named equivalence class. Sibling cluster 1 also lives in representation learning but its tactic is architectural mechanism design (instantiating a structural prior as a dual-branch distributional-divergence module); it adds a mechanism, not a proof that the latent structure is recoverable. Sibling cluster 29 likewise 'establishes a new property,' yet its object is a projection operator's descent-implies-distance property in constrained optimization, yielding stability classifications of stationary points — a guarantee about optimization iterates, not about recovering ground-truth latent factors. Clusters 11 and 19 also pivot assumptions, but to tighten privacy-composition and distribution-free generalization bounds respectively, again not to identify hidden structure. The signature of cluster 5 versus all of these is the phrase 'recoverable up to permutation/sign/linear transform' as the proof's terminal claim.

## When to pick this one

Pick this when your goal is to recover hidden/latent/causal/generative structure and the bottleneck is an existing identifiability result whose precondition fails in your setting. The decisive signal is that a different KIND of constraint already present in your data or architecture (a structural separability, a geometric signature, sparsity, sequential context) plausibly carries the identifying information the prior distributional or parametric assumption was supplying.


---C11---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster pivots the assumption embedded in a discrete or sequential choice mechanism — which elements enter or leave a budgeted selection/query procedure — and rebuilds that mechanism's performance certificate. Sibling cluster 19 follows the same outer 'replace-then-rebound' shape but acts on a continuous estimator: it swaps a hard operator (truncation) for a smooth dampening operator inside a fixed variance-reducing correction term and re-derives the generalization bound on the same data the estimator already consumes. The sharp contrast is the OBJECT of the pivot — cluster 11 alters a combinatorial/sequential rule about which items are picked, retained, or queried (and frequently reframes selection into a different problem class like stopping or control), whereas cluster 19 alters the analytic form of an estimator without changing what data flows into it. Where cluster 19 stays inside one estimator family, cluster 11's hallmark move is changing the problem class the choice belongs to.

## When to pick this one

Pick this when your problem is fundamentally a choice mechanism under a budget — which items, candidates, or queries to keep/acquire, which component to route to, or when to stop — and you suspect the rule's criterion (what it greedily optimizes or ranks by) or a structural precondition it requires (a fixed count, an always-present anchor, proximity to a reference) encodes an assumption that real settings can weaken or that a better-matched problem class would dissolve.


---C19---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster is the relax-and-re-prove-a-statistical-bound branch of the parent: it weakens a regularity/independence/boundedness condition embedded inside a probabilistic certificate and re-derives the guarantee, never constructing a counterexample or exploit on the violate side. Its nearest sibling is cluster 11, which also tightens probabilistic bounds, but cluster 11 works by re-modeling an accounting/composition mechanism for repeated resource-bounded trials over a shared sensitive pool — it reframes how costs accumulate rather than identifying a weaker sufficient condition for an assumption a bound rests on. Cluster 5, by contrast, establishes exact algebraic equivalences between two model formulations via reparameterization, a duality move with no assumption being relaxed at all, and cluster 29 proves a new structural property of an operator rather than weakening a precondition of a known result. The distinguishing signature here is that the pivoted object is a proof-internal assumption of a concentration/generalization/regret/query-complexity statement, and the payoff is either regime-extension or a strictly smaller leading term.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the load-bearing assumption sits inside a probabilistic guarantee — a concentration, generalization, regret, or sample-complexity bound — and is a regularity, independence, boundedness, or single-distribution condition the proof imported for convenience that a weaker sufficient property can still close. Choose a sibling instead if you are reframing how a resource cost accumulates, establishing an equivalence between formulations, or proving a new operator property rather than slackening a precondition.


---C28---
## Differentiation within parent

Cluster 19 also pivots a hard operator into a smooth one (truncation → dampening), but it does so for a single estimator on one machine and re-derives a distribution-free generalization bound that then becomes the end-to-end training objective; this cluster keeps the same kind of operator swap (and several other pivot forms) inside a multi-node protocol and re-derives convergence or sample–communication bounds whose operative variables are nodes, synchronization rounds, participation, and inter-agent heterogeneity rather than a single estimator's variance. It further differs from cluster 29, whose pivot establishes a new property of the projection operator within single-agent constrained optimization, and from cluster 11, which pivots accounting/composition bounds for repeated privacy-sensitive trials over a shared pool; here the assumption under audit is the architecture of distributed iterative updates — who computes, who corrects, and when they synchronize — not a constraint-set geometry or a privacy ledger.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when your gap lives inside a multi-node optimization protocol (federated, decentralized, or multi-agent) and the load-bearing assumption is about the protocol's system structure — where a mechanism resides, whether local work is harm or resource, whether two events must be coupled — rather than a property of a single estimator, a single-machine operator, or a privacy-accounting ledger.


---C29---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster pivots the assumption of a *convergence/regret guarantee for an iterative optimization dynamics* and keeps the procedure essentially fixed, changing only the analytical primitive (regularity condition, convergence target, or measurement criterion) to re-prove a rate. Sibling cluster 19 also lives in the optimization/learning-theory space but its move is to *modify the operator itself* — replacing a hard truncation with a smooth dampening — and then derive a distribution-free *generalization* bound that it optimizes as the training objective; the target is statistical (generalization) and the algorithm is altered, whereas here the target is dynamics (rate/separation) and the algorithm is held constant. It is likewise distinct from sibling cluster 28, whose move is architectural decoupling of distributed optimization into a local update and an aggregation-side optimizer rather than auditing a load-bearing assumption of an existing proof.

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the artifact whose assumption you are auditing is a convergence or regret guarantee for an iterative update procedure, the load-bearing assumption is a regularity condition or a convergence-target/measurement criterion, and the payoff you seek is a rate, a class separation, or coverage of a harder regime — not a generalization bound or a new architectural mechanism.


---C09---
## Differentiation within parent

All four clusters substitute a representation, but what the substitution is chosen to preserve differs sharply. Here the surrogate is selected for its bounded per-element influence, and the essential property preserved is a formal privacy/confidentiality/adaptive-correctness guarantee while utility is held at the non-private baseline — the noise or verification mechanism is physically relocated onto the low-sensitivity intermediate. Sibling cluster 14 substitutes representation to preserve and expand the expressivity of pairwise interactions (splitting a unit into content and position vectors and re-deriving the interaction score), where sensitivity to individual records is irrelevant. Sibling cluster 30 reparameterizes a non-convex factored formulation to preserve the optimum while reshaping the optimization landscape, a move aimed at trainability rather than disclosure cost. The distinguishing signature of cluster 9 is that the surrogate's selling point is a provable cap on how much one input element can move it, and the payoff is a privacy-utility (or verification-cost) frontier, not an expressivity or optimization-landscape gain.

## When to pick this one

Pick this when the bottleneck is a per-element sensitivity, disclosure, or verification cost that scales with ambient dimension, runtime, or solution-set size, and there is (or can be built) a compact object the computation routes through whose per-element influence is provably bounded. The tell is a formal privacy/confidentiality/adaptive-adversary constraint where the cost is an artifact of attaching the mechanism to the raw input or final output rather than to that intermediate.


---C12---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster targets the macro-structural plumbing of deep compositional networks — parameter locus, skip/routing topology, and per-stage aggregation primitive — whereas sibling cluster 14 stays *inside* a single interaction score, disentangling it into factored content×position cross-product terms; cluster 12 papers rewire how stages connect and where parameters sit rather than decomposing one pairwise scoring function. Against cluster 30, which reparameterizes a non-convex *estimation/optimization* problem with a degree-indexed hierarchy to reshape the loss landscape, cluster 12's substitutions act on the forward computation graph itself (which operator aggregates, which connection carries the signal) and keep the network end-to-end trainable, not on the geometry of an estimator's objective. And unlike cluster 9, which reformulates *certification* by shifting from post-hoc artifact inspection to procedure attestation, cluster 12 never touches verification — it swaps the building blocks that produce the forward output.

## When to pick this one

Pick this when the bottleneck lives in a staged network's structural plumbing — where trainable parameters sit, how stages connect, or what operator aggregates per stage — and you can name a classical-theorem-grounded or bijectively-equivalent surrogate that fits that exact slot while flipping a cost or expressivity property. Choose a scoring-function decomposition (14) instead if the bottleneck is inside a single interaction term, or an estimator reparameterization (30) if it is in the optimization landscape rather than the forward graph.


---C14---
## Differentiation within parent

This cluster physically removes the operator and installs a cheaper primitive in its place, whereas sibling cluster 12 keeps the module intact and instead re-explains its mechanism — using Hessian curvature spectra to argue landscape flattening, not the conventionally attributed cause, drives generalization; that is a reinterpretation move, the opposite of a substitution. It also differs from sibling cluster 30, whose degree-parameterized reparameterization builds an infinite hierarchy of non-convex estimation problems to ease optimization/identifiability — a reparameterization of an estimation objective, not a cost-driven swap of a forward relational or sequence operator. The defining signature here is a sub-quadratic surrogate for the dense pairwise-interaction operator whose acceptance hinges on a preserved-capacity proof (expressivity, receptive field, retrieval capacity, or order-preservation), rather than on a new mechanistic explanation (cluster 12) or an estimation-tractability reparameterization (cluster 30).

## When to pick this one

Pick this instantiation when the bottleneck is concretely the dense pairwise-interaction (all-pairs / softmax-attention) operator over N elements and you can name a cheaper primitive whose preserved property — expressivity class, receptive field, or retrieval capacity — you can prove or exactly equate. Do not pick it when the operator's cost is acceptable but its mechanism is misunderstood (a re-explanation move) or when the expense lives in estimation/optimization rather than the forward interaction.


---C30---
## Differentiation within parent

Sibling cluster 12 also wields Hessian eigenvalue spectra, but it keeps an existing forward-pass module intact and uses curvature as a *diagnostic lens* to re-attribute that module's generalization to landscape flattening — curvature is the explanatory instrument, the module is unchanged. Cluster 30 instead makes the curvature/second-order object of the *optimization dynamics itself* the thing it substitutes, approximates, or reshapes, and its deliverable is an operational guarantee (saddle escape, conditioning-decoupled convergence, scalable attribution, conserved-quantity completeness) rather than a mechanistic re-attribution. It is likewise distinct from cluster 14, whose substituted object is a forward-pass representation (dual content/position vectors with disentangled interaction terms) in service of expressivity, not a second-order object in service of training-dynamics behavior. In short: 12 reads curvature to reinterpret a layer, 14 swaps the representation inside the forward map, and 30 substitutes or re-conditions the curvature operator that drives learning.

## When to pick this one

Choose this instantiation when your bottleneck lives in the second-order structure of the training dynamics — the Hessian/NTK/Fisher spectrum, ill-conditioning, spurious-minima or saddle geometry, or curvature-based scoring — rather than in a forward-pass operator's runtime or a representation's expressive capacity. The tell is that the quantity you need to control is a spectral property of how learning proceeds, not of what the network computes on a single input.


